win32u: end the SC_MOVE/SC_SIZE loop when the moved window is destroyed.

An application can complete the operation itself while sys_command_size_move is
still looping. Affinity's dockable panels do exactly that: the panel docks as
soon as it is dragged over a dock target -- not when the button is released --
and its floating window is destroyed there and then.

Traced with +message,+win while dragging a panel into a tab strip. The floating
window 0x200a8 gets WM_CLOSE and is destroyed 670 log events before the button
comes up; the loop keeps running on the dead handle for all of them, and the
WM_EXITSIZEMOVE sent afterwards appears nowhere in the message trace because
there is no longer a window to send it to.

Two visible consequences, both long-standing complaints:

  - the loop goes on calling NtUserSetWindowPos on the destroyed window, which
    pulls the panel back out of the dock it just entered -- "it docks, then
    jumps back out unless you jiggle the mouse"
  - the loop swallows the WM_LBUTTONUP (NtUserGetMessage removes it and the
    loop breaks without dispatching), so the application never takes down its
    own drag adornment. The dragged tab stays drawn where it was dropped, on
    top of the strip's real tabs, until an unrelated click delivers the input
    that finishes the gesture. That is the "click another tab to fix it"
    workaround users report.

Windows ends the move when the window goes away. Do the same, and dispatch the
message that revealed it rather than dropping it -- in the observed case that
message is the private one WPF uses to drain its dispatcher queue, and losing
it leaves the application in a worse state than the bug being fixed.

Verified against Affinity 3 on the Intel Arc: three consecutive drags of a panel
into an existing tab strip, each ending with a correctly laid out and fully
repainted strip, where every previous attempt left it drawn mid-drag. A plain
move of a floating panel that does not dock still tracks the cursor exactly, and
tools/kbmove.c confirms the keyboard path still moves the window and still exits
on Return.

--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -676,7 +676,7 @@
 static void sys_command_size_move( HWND hwnd, WPARAM wparam )
 {
     DWORD msg_pos = NtUserGetMessagePos();
-    BOOL thickframe, drag_full_windows = TRUE, moved = FALSE, mouse_drag;
+    BOOL thickframe, drag_full_windows = TRUE, moved = FALSE, mouse_drag, window_gone = FALSE;
     RECT sizing_rect, mouse_rect, orig_rect;
     UINT hittest = wparam & 0x0f;
     UINT syscommand = wparam & 0xfff0;
@@ -788,6 +788,28 @@
         if (!NtUserGetMessage( &msg, 0, 0, 0 )) break;
         if (NtUserCallMsgFilter( &msg, MSGF_SIZE )) continue;
 
+        /* The application can finish the operation itself while we are still
+         * looping. A dockable panel docks the moment it is dragged over a dock
+         * target and destroys its floating window there and then, without
+         * waiting for the button to come up. Windows ends the move when the
+         * window being moved goes away; we kept going, driving a destroyed
+         * window with SetWindowPos -- which pulled the panel straight back out
+         * of the dock -- and then swallowed the WM_LBUTTONUP the application
+         * needed to take down its own drag adornment, leaving the tab strip
+         * that received the drop drawn mid-drag until some later click. */
+        if (!is_window( hwnd ))
+        {
+            TRACE( "window %p was destroyed, ending the move at msg %04x\n", hwnd, msg.message );
+            /* deliver the message we just took: the application is in the middle
+             * of its own bookkeeping and swallowing it here loses, among other
+             * things, the private message a UI toolkit uses to drain its
+             * dispatcher queue. */
+            NtUserTranslateMessage( &msg, 0 );
+            NtUserDispatchMessage( &msg );
+            window_gone = TRUE;
+            break;
+        }
+
         /* An application can consume the WM_LBUTTONUP itself: a dockable panel
          * accepting a drop does exactly that, as does a window that is
          * re-parented or destroyed as part of the drop. The check further down
@@ -914,6 +936,12 @@
 
     set_capture_window( 0, GUI_INMOVESIZE, NULL );
     NtUserReleaseDC( parent, hdc );
+
+    /* None of the below means anything for a window that no longer exists: the
+     * two send_message calls would be silently dropped and the CBT hook would
+     * be handed a dead handle. Say so rather than relying on that. */
+    if (window_gone) return;
+
     if (parent) map_window_points( 0, parent, (POINT *)&sizing_rect, 2, get_thread_dpi() );
 
     if (call_hooks( WH_CBT, HCBT_MOVESIZE, (WPARAM)hwnd, (LPARAM)&sizing_rect, sizeof(sizing_rect) ))
